G e n e r a l   A l g e b r a i c   M o d e l i n g   S y s t e m
C o m p i l a t i o n


      CEE 6410
      Example 6.3 in the Bishop Et al text https://digitalcommons.usu.edu/ecstatic_all/76/ with an
      additional $35,000 upfront costs to establish the wholesale contract (pay laywers :)
       
      A water wholesaler is willing to sell water to your municipal water supply utility in an
      amount not exceeding 12,000 ac-ft per year, but only if you buy at least 1,000 ac-ft/yr
      (to amortize their pipe cost).  Water from the wholesaler will cost $150/ac-ft.
       
      An alternative source of water is to construct your own treatment plant.
      The minimum sized plant which will meet your demand of 2,000 ac-ft/yr will cost $90,000 annual
      capital cost and $120/ac-ft to operate.
       
      With the additional $35,000 capital cost for establishing the wholesale contract.
       
      Allows user to control bounds on "integer" variables (IntUpBnd and IntLowBnd).
       
      Presently fully relaxed.
       
      David E Rosenberg
      david.rosenberg@usu.edu
      Updated October 10, 2026
  24   
  25  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  26  SETS src water supply sources /tp "treatment plant", wc "wholesale contract"/;
  27   
  28  * 2. DEFINE input data
  29  PARAMETERS
  30     CapCost(src) capital cost ($ to build)
  31           /tp 90000,
  32            wc 35000/
  33     OpCost(src) operating cost ($ per ac-ft)
  34           /tp 120
  35            wc 150/
  36     MaxCapacity(src) Maximum capacity of source when built (ac-ft per year)
  37            /tp 2000,
  38             wc  12000/
  39     MinUse(src) Minimum required use of source when built (ac-ft per year)
  40            /tp 0,
  41             wc  1000/
  42     TotDemand  Total Demand (ac-ft per year) /2000/
  43   
  44   
  45  * "Integer" variables free within 0 to 1 bounds.
  46  * Allows user to relax, tighten bounds on integer variables
  47  * Presently set in fully relaxed mode
  48     IntUpBnd(src) Upper bound on integer variables (#)
  49            /tp 1,
  50             wc 1 /
  51   
  52  IntLowBnd(src) Lower bound on integer variables (#)
  53             /tp 0,
  54             wc 0/ ;
  55   
  56  * 3. DEFINE the variables
  57  VARIABLES I(src) binary decision to build or do prject from source src (1=yes 0=no)
  58            X(src) volume of water provided by source src (ac-ft per year)
  59            TCOST  total capital and operating costs of supply actions ($);
  60   
  61  * Non-negativity constraints
  62  POSITIVE VARIABLES X;
  63   
  64  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  65  EQUATIONS
  66     COST            Total Cost ($) and objective function value
  67     MaxCap(src)     Maximum capacity of source when built (ac-ft per year)
  68     MinReqUse(src)  Minimum required use of source when built (ac-ft per year)
  69     MeetDemand      Meet demand (ac-ft per year)
  70     IntUpBound(src) Upper bound on interger variables (number)
  71     IntLowBound(src) Lower bound on integer variables (number);
  72   
  73  COST..                 TCOST =E= SUM(src,CapCost(src)*I(src) + OpCost(src)*X(src));
  74  MaxCap(src) ..           X(src) =L= MaxCapacity(src)*I(src);
  75  MinReqUse(src) ..        X(src) =G= MinUse(src)*I(src);
  76  MeetDemand ..            sum(src,X(src)) =G= TotDemand;
  77  IntUpBound(src) ..       I(src) =L= IntUpBnd(src);
  78  IntLowBound(src) ..      I(src) =G= IntLowBnd(src);
  79   
  80  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
  81  MODEL WatSupplyRelaxed /ALL/;
  82   
  83  option solprint = on;
  84   
  85  * 6. Solve the Model as an LP (relaxed IP)
  86  SOLVE WatSupplyRelaxed USING LP MINIMIZING TCOST;
  87   
  88  DISPLAY X.L, I.L, TCOST.L;
  89   
      * Dump all input data and results to a GAMS gdx file
      Execute_Unload "Ex6-3-relaxed.gdx";
      * Dump the gdx file to an Excel workbook
      Execute "gdx2xls Ex6-3.relaxed.gdx"


